WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.833
It is three in the morning.

00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:07.666
A user named Mickey taps Pay, nine dollars and ninety-nine cents, for a book on a Stripe checkout.

00:00:07.666 --> 00:00:10.498
Half a second later he is looking at a receipt.

00:00:10.498 --> 00:00:13.331
In that half second, real money moved.

00:00:13.331 --> 00:00:16.164
Stripe moves about a trillion dollars a year, one tap like this at a time.

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:18.997
You already know the four letters that keep that tap honest.

00:00:18.997 --> 00:00:21.830
Atomicity.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.663
Consistency.

00:00:24.663 --> 00:00:27.495
Isolation.

00:00:27.495 --> 00:00:30.328
Durability.

00:00:30.328 --> 00:00:33.161
Watch them work on this one charge.

00:00:33.161 --> 00:00:36.934
That one tap is four writes to the database.

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:40.708
Stripe asks Visa to charge the card.

00:00:40.708 --> 00:00:44.481
It records the payment in a ledger, and that ledger uses double entry, so the payment is two rows that must sum to zero.

00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:48.254
It bumps the merchant's running balance.

00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:52.027
And it queues a webhook so the merchant knows to ship the book.

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:55.800
Charge the card, write the ledger, bump the balance, queue the webhook.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.574
Four writes, hiding behind one button.

00:00:59.574 --> 00:01:03.347
Now watch what happens when they go wrong.

00:01:03.347 --> 00:01:06.823
Take Mickey's charge.

00:01:06.823 --> 00:01:10.300
Stripe charges his card, writes the ledger, and then the connection drops before the webhook is queued.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.776
The card charged but the rest never landed.

00:01:13.776 --> 00:01:17.252
Money left, no book shipped.

00:01:17.252 --> 00:01:20.729
That partial charge is atomicity's job, all of it or none of it.

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:24.205
Next failure.

00:01:24.205 --> 00:01:27.681
A bug in Mickey's ledger writes nine ninety-nine on one row and nine ninety-eight on the other.

00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:31.158
The two rows no longer sum to zero.

00:01:31.158 --> 00:01:34.634
A ledger that stops balancing is consistency's job, the database refuses to invent a cent.

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:38.111
Third.

00:01:38.111 --> 00:01:41.587
Two charges hit the same merchant balance at the same instant.

00:01:41.587 --> 00:01:45.063
Both read the balance, both add their amount, both write it back.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:48.540
Predict what the balance shows.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.016
One update is lost.

00:01:52.016 --> 00:01:55.492
The second write lands on the stale read and erases the first.

00:01:55.492 --> 00:01:58.969
That lost update is isolation's job.

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:02.445
Run the two as if one ran after the other, and no money vanishes.

00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:05.921
Last failure.

00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:09.398
The receipt prints, and five milliseconds later a power event takes the server down.

00:02:09.398 --> 00:02:12.874
A confirmed charge that vanishes in a crash is durability's job.

00:02:12.874 --> 00:02:17.955
So that is ACID, running in production at three in the morning.

00:02:17.955 --> 00:02:23.035
The partial charge, the ledger that drifts, the lost update, the receipt the crash forgets.

00:02:23.035 --> 00:02:28.116
Four ways money goes missing, four guarantees that stop it.

00:02:28.116 --> 00:02:33.196
Now we stop reading about transactions and start writing them.

00:02:33.196 --> 00:02:38.277
The next page does each one in plain SQL, with BEGIN and COMMIT around the writes that have to land together.
